<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05TP">Remark <span data-tag="05TP">7.7.2.11</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category which admits pullbacks and let </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[ \operatorname{ev}_{1}: \operatorname{Fun}(\Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}} \]
</div>
<p> denote the cartesian fibration given by evaluation at the vertex $1 \in \Delta ^1$. It follows from Notation <a data-tag="05TN" href="/tag/05TN">7.7.2.10</a> that a morphism $F: K^{\triangleright } \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a descent diagram for $\operatorname{ev}_{1}$ if and only if the restriction functor </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \theta : \operatorname{Fun}( K^{\triangleright }, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})^{\operatorname{Cart}}_{/F} \rightarrow \operatorname{Fun}( K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})^{\operatorname{Cart}}_{/F|_{K}} \]
</div>
<p> is fully faithful. Similarly $F$ is an effective descent diagram for $\operatorname{ev}_{1}$ if and only if $\theta $ is an equivalence of $\infty $-categories. </p>
</article>
